The shadow comparison varies the ENGINE and the WORLD at the same time, so
a difference cannot be attributed to either. The 6h staleness gate tried to
hold the world still by proxy; on prod that left 0 comparable pairs out of
300. Age is the wrong proxy in both directions — it discards a 200h-old
decision on a game where nothing changed, and accepts a 20-minute-old one on
a game rescanned in between.

Replay holds the world still directly: reconstruct the inputs the legacy
engine had at its decision time, run the same imported economics against
them, compare. Every decision already recorded becomes a test case, offline,
instead of waiting days per lane for fresh pairs across 34 lanes.

MarketResearchSnapshot turns out to keep exactly what this needs — demandScore,
sellers and `at`, one row per scan, 120 days — and SaleSignal is append-only,
so window drift is two existence queries.

Scoped to the sellability stage on purpose. Pool depth, capacity, host state
and archive coverage are not snapshotted anywhere, so decisions downstream of
that gate are reported as out of scope rather than scored. Measuring the
reconstruction's blind spots and calling it engine disagreement is the same
mistake the 6h gate made.

No economics reimplemented: demandAllocation, salesOf, capForGame and
marketStockFloor are all imported. Where that discipline costs fidelity —
reconstructing a past avgPrice would mean copying the SaleSignal aggregation —
the row is reported unreplayable instead of guessed. Only exact-fidelity rows
count as evidence.

Two pre-existing findings surfaced while building it, both documented:

  * AutoFarmTask.demandScore is the raw market score on most rows but the
    blended effective demand on skip_low_demand/skip_probe_budget, where
    hadResearch is also hardcoded true even with no research at all.
  * the lane's decision vocabulary is a strict subset of the legacy engine's.
    decide.js has no time gate, coverage gate, pool-floor, capacity,
    host-offline or reuse-only gate, so a live lane would spend on campaigns
    the legacy engine correctly declines. Unobserved so far because the
    comparison is broken — zero comparable pairs, zero chance to notice.
    Not fixed here; named as lane_missing_gate so it stops reading as noise.

Also: decide.js had the action-class mapping written out twice in the same
file. Both now come from decisionClasses.js, and an unclassified decision
returns "unknown" rather than falling through to "skip" — the old default
would have made a new spend-class decision agree with an idle lane.

autoFarmer.js and autoLister.js are untouched, not even an added export.
laneReadiness keeps every existing blocker; requireReplay defaults to false
so this cannot silently raise the bar on a live promotion path.

24 new tests; the 63 existing farm2 tests pass unmodified. Suite 638/643 vs
a 614/619 baseline — the same 5 pre-existing orphan-test failures either side.

Proposal only. Nothing here is verified against production; the open
questions are in docs/FARM2-VERIFICATION.md section 7.
